WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.260
The hardest loop defects are often data-context defects rather than connection defects.

2
00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:18.440
A loop can have the correct node, the correct return path, and the correct batch size, yet still update the wrong record because the item no longer carries the identifier that connects the result to the source.

3
00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:23.500
Reliable loop design treats data identity as a first-class part of the workflow.

4
00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:37.380
In a customer-enrichment loop, preserve fields such as customerId, sourceIndex, requestId, the original input object when needed, the external API result, processing status, and error information.

5
00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:40.760
The exact field names can vary, but the principle is stable.

6
00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:48.220
Later nodes should not depend on a fragile assumption that the original record will remain at the top level after every transformation.

7
00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:51.700
External responses frequently reshape data.

8
00:00:51.800 --> 00:01:00.040
An HTTP Request node may return a response body whose top-level fields belong to the external service, not to the original customer item.

9
00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:05.020
A normalization step may replace the JSON object with a smaller destination-ready shape.

10
00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:08.160
A code step may map values into a new structure.

11
00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:14.980
If the original identifier is not carried forward deliberately, the workflow may lose the link between the enriched data and the source record.

12
00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:24.860
Item linking exists in n8n to preserve relationships between items as workflows transform data, but an operator should still make important business identifiers explicit.

13
00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:32.320
A visible customerId or requestId is easier to inspect, log, compare, and use in a destination upsert.

14
00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:42.100
It also makes recovery easier after a partial failure because the workflow can identify which records succeeded, which records failed, and which records can be retried safely.

15
00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:48.460
A practical pattern is to build a normalized working object early in the loop body.

16
00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:56.400
Put the source identifier, source index, request identifier, original values needed later, and a status field in that object.

17
00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:00.660
Then let enrichment and destination fields accumulate beside those values.

18
00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:06.440
This avoids a fragile chain of assumptions about which node last owned the top-level JSON shape.

19
00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:11.600
The done output marks the distinction between per-iteration data and the complete processed set.

20
00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:17.140
Inside the loop body, the workflow works on the item or batch currently released through the loop output.

21
00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:22.040
After the loop finishes, a summary on the done path can reason about the finished set.

22
00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:33.900
That summary should be based on explicit status fields such as processed, skipped, failed, and retried rather than on the assumption that every released item reached the destination.

23
00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:38.500
Loop Over Items also exposes context values that can help diagnostics.

24
00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:48.200
The documented expression {{$("Loop Over Items").context["noItemsLeft"]}} returns a boolean that indicates whether the node has no more data to process.

25
00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:56.380
The documented expression {{$("Loop Over Items").context["currentRunIndex"];}} returns the current running index of the node.

26
00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:03.200
These values can support logging, diagnostics, controlled status messages, or carefully designed conditions.

27
00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:06.440
Context expressions do not replace business identity.

28
00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:15.020
A run index can tell an operator where the loop was in its sequence, but it does not prove which customer, ticket, invoice, or file was processed.

29
00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:21.440
Use loop context to understand execution state, and use stable identifiers to protect the meaning of the data.

30
00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:25.300
When both are present, troubleshooting becomes much more concrete.

31
00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:28.460
Data integrity also affects the done path.

32
00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:34.240
A final summary should not assume that every item reaching the loop output completed successfully.

33
00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:38.820
It should aggregate explicit status fields from the processed set.

34
00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:43.960
That makes the summary resilient to skips, handled errors, and retry outcomes.

35
00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:53.340
It also gives downstream monitoring a stable contract: the workflow can report exactly how many records reached each state and which identifiers require follow-up.

36
00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:55.940
This approach pays off during recovery.

37
00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:07.720
If an execution stops halfway through a large job, the team can inspect the preserved identifiers and statuses, isolate unfinished records, and restart only the work that still needs attention.

38
00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:15.260
Without that context, the only choices are manual investigation or risky replay of operations that may already have succeeded.
